What I Learned from This Assignment**

This assignment helped me understand how to build a backend application using Django and Django REST Framework. Before starting this project 
I had some knowledge of Python and web development.. This project showed me how the different parts of a real application connect and work 
together. I learned about models, serializers, views, APIs, databases, testing and validation. It was all very hands-on. Helped me see how 
everything fits together in a real-world system.

**1. Django Framework**

I learned how to create Django models and use them to represent database tables. I worked with models like Product, Box, Order and OrderItem. 
Understood how they connect through relationships. I saw how ViewSets make it easier to create CRUD APIs with minimal code. I also learned how 
serializers help convert database objects into API responses and validate input data. I got familiar with URL routing, Django Admin and how 
database migrations keep the database structure up to date.


**2. REST API Development**

This project gave me experience in building a RESTful API. I learned what CRUD operations are. Create, Read, Update and Delete. And when to 
use HTTP methods like GET and POST. I practiced creating custom API actions, like selecting the right box for an order. I also learned how
important it is to send error messages and use proper status codes like 400 and 404. Providing data. Such as box dimensions, weight and 
utilization. Made the API responses much clearer and easier to use.

**3. Testing**

Testing was a part of this project and taught me a lot. I learned the difference between unit testing and integration testing. Unit tests
helped me check pieces like models and the algorithm logic. Integration tests helped me see if the full API worked together as expected. I
realized how important it is to test edge cases. Not the usual situations. The 21 test cases I wrote helped me discover bugs andmade the 
application more reliable. I now understand that testing is not optional. It’s essential.

**4. Database and Data Modeling**

I learned how to design a database structure based on requirements. I used relationships like ForeignKey and ManyToMany. I also learned when 
a through model is needed. Especially when you need to store information in a relationship. I used validators to make sure only correct data 
gets into the database. I created helper methods to calculate things like product volume and total order weight. This helped keep the code

clean and the logic clear.

**5. Code Quality and Development Practices**

This project taught me that writing code is not about making it work. It’s also about making it clean, readable, maintainable and well-documented.
I learned to separate responsibilities. Like keeping models, serializers and views independent. I understood why documentation matters. It helps
others (and my future self) understand what the code does. I also got better at using Git for version control. I used a Makefile to make common 
tasks easier, like running tests or starting the server. I learned that proper error handling makes the application more stable and user-friendly.

**6. Problem-Solving**

The challenging part was the box selection algorithm. I had to consider dimensions, rotation, weight limits, multiple products and efficiency 
at once. The best way to solve this was to break the problem into pieces. I worked on each condition separately. Like checking if a product 
fits in a box, testing rotation then checking weight limits. I tested different scenarios. This helped me find where the algorithm failed and
how to fix it. I learned that solving problems step by step is much more effective than trying to do everything at once.

**What Surprised Me**

What surprised me the most was how a task. Like choosing a box for an order. Could get so complicated. I didn’t expect many constraints to 
affect the result. I also realized that handling edge cases is just as important as building the feature. Sometimes the hardest parts are not 
the ones but the ones that don’t happen often. But still need to be managed.

**Key Learnings**

The main things I learned from this assignment are:

- Django makes backend and API development easier and faster.

- Algorithms must consider all constraints and edge cases.

- Testing is essential to find bugs and improve reliability.

- Clean code and clear documentation make projects easier to maintain.

- Breaking a problem into smaller parts makes it much easier to solve.

**Future Improvements**

If I kept working on this project I would improve the box selection algorithm. I would look into advanced 3D packing methods. I could also add 
customer preferences, different shipping costs and pricing tiers. I could even connect with shipping APIs to get live rates. For a system I
would focus on performance, logging, monitoring and handling a large number of orders. These improvements would make the application more 
practical and scalable.

**Overall Reflection**

Overall this assignment gave me experience in Django, REST APIs, database design, algorithms, testing and software development practices. It 
helped me move beyond theory. See how all these pieces come together in a working application. It was not about learning one skill. It was 
about understanding how they connect. This project improved my problem-solving skills. Gave me a better feel, for how real backend systems are
built, tested and maintained. It was challenging, but very rewarding.
